ndment and ratify it  which they did  and the proposition thereby becomes a  part of the constitution  of course you could i don't see anything  in the constitution that would prohibit  a counter initiative if that's what  you're saying if i'm understanding you  correctly  i don't think that's likely to happen we  don't have much of a history of  counter initiatives in the way that we  do have the 18th and 21st amendments to  the united states comp  constitution having to do with  prohibition which pretty much follow  that kind of sequence the 21st amendment  basically undoing the 18th amendment and  cancelling out  prohibition  but i i do think  we're not going to see any any  rectifying  or  remedying a constitutional proposal  until the people really understand some  aspects of the current amendment a  little bit better than they perhaps do  right now  i think if the average person in the  state of michigan  were to read the university of michigan  report or read the critique in the  hillsdale report of the michigan report  they would really uh  gasp at the  incongruity  of the proposals of the university of  michigan with their own perception of  what it was that they were voting for in  2018  i think would be extraordinarily  interesting if there was some kind of  poll  to uh get some sense of how the people  perceive the kinds of things that the  university of michigan is proposing in  facilitating their new theory of  representation  i really think that  you you can walk across the streets of  any community in this country in this  state  you can describe to them what the  universe university of michigan is  proposing and i think there will be  gasps and disbelief that somehow they  have contributed to placing in the  constitution  such a utter transformation of our  constitutional system  not only the explicit consideration of  race and ethnicity and religion  but the  the  total  derogation  of  everything that's traditionally guided  this this  this practi