  what advice would you give to these  quote-unquote independent commissioners  um to to make sure their work is as  independent as possible um the second  question and dr brustell was starting to  get into this a little bit towards the  end of his comments um does does what  what's happening here in michigan and  what could possibly happen across the  nation  i know the for the people act is kind of  stalled in congress but  if it were enacted it would require  independent independent commissions like  this one in michigan in every state in  the country  does this constitute regime change uh dr  bostel was getting into you know what  republicanism means what representation  means uh for the the framers of the  constitution this sounds wholly  different um is is this a new regime  altogether what would the country look  like what what would our politics look  like uh where this should be adopted on  a mass scale thank you  yeah so i mean i think  the first question  um  you know  about gerrymandering i mean i think  there are a lot of  myths about gerrymandering and its  effect on american politics  most people think that polarization and  gerrymandering are somehow connected or  that gerrymandering is driving a lot of  what's happening in our politics today  the better explanation for what's  happening in our politics is that we are  sorting ourselves into like-minded  communities so  you would have to in order to create  sort of moderate  representatives you would have to  gerrymander districts in order to put  people who think differently back  together given the ways in which people  have moved into places where people  think like them so  and it does lead to the second question  right what would our national politics  look when we go down this path i think  we've already seen it i think we're  catching glimpses of it already  it's every four years this is the most  important election of your lifetime  um scorched earth battles  to win 51 to 49  majorities for one office it gets to  make all of the decisions bare  majoritarianism that kind of thing so  that isn't a recipe for compromise and  deliberation and mutual accommodation  and so that's why i think it's really  important to pause at a juncture like  this and think about the future we want  to have politically  we have time for one more question  and this  young man here will ask it  uh thank you for each of your talks and  i thought they were very enlightening  um right now i'm taking american  political thought and we spent the past  three weeks talking about the  anti-federalists and their views on  representation and i'm just curious on  how this new  vision of representation would compare  to traditional anti-federalist  thought i'm sorry i couldn't clearly  hear that final that final presentation  of the question could you just repeat  that please i'm just curious on how this  new vision of representation would  compare to anti-federalist visions of  representation  i mean i would say succinctly when i  think of the federal anti-federalist i  think of  small republicanism  right citizen participation stress on  virtue when i think about this form of  representation  it really emerges in the i mean you  always have interest groups that play an  important role in american history but  this form of representation really  emerges the 1960s  gene kirkpatrick who was a democrat  herself in 1974 talked about this new  form of representation that you find in  the democrat party as part of the  mcgovern commission 1972  where there are de facto racial quotas  that will be included in representation  among the delegates  the basic point was is in order to  overthrow that kind of mid-century  liberal  color-blind view which they viewed as  dishonest  because america was systemically racist  what you needed to do was to represent  explicitly certain gender and race  groups  um and you call those the new states if  you will right so this is the way we  ought to understand the american  electorate i think anti-federalists they  have a very different view of  republicanism  and  despite their differences with the  federalists on how large the regime  would be republicanism really was the  common thread that ran through  both sides of that disagreement  and so republicanism not just as a kind  of forum but literally as a kind of soul  where people participated very  intimately in local politics  fast forward to today and what do we  have we literally have the democrat  candidates talking about doing away with  the electoral college and we have  republicans in michigan who are voting  for michigan to allocate its electors  based on the popular vote so in other  words to make michigan irrelevant in  national politics  that has nothing in common with  republicanism at all  yeah i i wanted to  just say one thing before we ended our  evening together here  and that is i think that  some of the people who have been most  poorly  treated by this process are the members  of the commission themselves  remember again these are citizens  who've been drawn randomly and placed on  a commission  they knew they were signing up for an  important responsibility a critical role  they were aware of that  but i don't think they were aware  that their job was going to go beyond  establishing regularly shaped districts  and diminishing partisanship and doing  their best doing their level best to  create  honest and fairly put together districts  in that their role would be expanded  beyond that  to assessing the future visions of  communities across the state of michigan  in trying to weigh those visions that  were best and that ought to be rewarded  with community of interest status  assessing legislative policies that do  or don't bind us together and again  trying to  reward those communities and those group  affinity groups and special interests  that favor certain policies i just don't  think these were the kinds of things  that most of these commission 